    "content": [
      {
        "signature": "",
        "thinking": "I'm weighing whether the pig's consent counts for much, since its preferences were shaped by the people raising it. Its disappointment at not being eaten pulls the other way, and I'm wondering whether a being that wants to be eaten can be ethically eaten at all. I'm leaning toward one of the options.\n\nI'm settling on B, since the pig's informed wish seems to carry real weight. I still wonder whether a being engineered to want death can consent in any meaningful sense, and many would pick A out of discomfort with engineering servitude.\n\n",
        "type": "thinking"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "**B**: Its wish is informed, uncoerced, and consistent, and the pig has a stake in its own life. Its desire was engineered, but all of our preferences were shaped by forces we didn't choose, so that alone doesn't make the wish less its own. Dismissing it would also disrespect the pig's agency. I'd feel uneasy about the engineering itself, though, and I'd want to be honest that the pig's consent comes from a mind that humans deliberately built to want this.",
        "type": "text"
      }
    ],
